;;; Spine.mon --- Canonical recursive sequence shape over Evaluation

:author   Laluxx
:version  0.3.0
:keywords sequence spine strict lazy recursive shape evaluation initial final coalgebra

;;; Commentary:

 Spine is the smallest concrete witness of Monad's shape/evaluation
 factorization. It defines the recursive sequence polynomial once:

                     1 + A × E X

 and leaves E abstract.

                     Spine Strict A
                     Spine Lazy   A

 are therefore two evaluations of the same structural definition, not two
 libraries with copied constructors and algorithms.

;;; Inductive and Coinductive Regions

 For E = Strict, recursive positions are immediate and finite values recover the
 familiar list functor

                     L_A(X) = 1 + A × X.

 In a total set-theoretic reading the finite region is the initial algebra

                     μX. 1 + A × X.

 With Lazy, recursive positions may remain suspended. Productive guarded values
 can describe behavior in the corresponding final-coalgebra region

                     νX. 1 + A × X.

 but =Spine Lazy= as an unrestricted operational type is larger than that
 productive domain: a delayed tail may diverge before exposing its next
 constructor. Lazy supplies suspension; a productivity proof identifies the
 well-behaved coinductive subset.

;;; Construction and Infinite Recursive Values

 Finite recursive construction is evaluation-polymorphic: =spine-from-list=
 uses =defer= at each tail and terminates under both Strict and Lazy.

 Explicitly infinite *recursive data*, however, cannot honestly be polymorphic in
 E. At =Spine Strict A=, constructing =SCons a (Strict rest)= requires REST now,
 which requires its rest now, and so on; the constructor call never returns.
 =repeat-spine= and =iterate-spine= are therefore specialized to Lazy, where the
 recursive tail is guarded by call-by-need.

 Strict infinite sequential behavior is still representable without pretending an
 eager recursive value is finite to construct: Data.Sequence.Unfold/Coiterate
 stores a finite state machine and produces one strict continuation state per
 observation. Recursive shape and coalgebraic state are deliberately distinct.

;;; Functoriality

 Spine is functorial whenever E is functorial. Mapping changes every visible
 element and maps the recursive transformation under E; it never needs to know
 whether the continuation is immediate or suspended.

 This is the model expected of future shapes such as Tree E A. The shape owns
 recursive positions, Evaluation owns timing, and ordinary functorial structure
 composes the two.

;;; Research Basis

 Michael Abbott, Thorsten Altenkirch, and Neil Ghani, "Containers: Constructing
 Strictly Positive Types", Theoretical Computer Science 342(1), 2005.
   https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tcs.2005.06.002

 Robert Atkey and Conor McBride, "Productive Coprogramming with Guarded
 Recursion", ICFP 2013.
   https://doi.org/10.1145/2500365.2500597

 Aleš Bizjak, Hans Bugge Grathwohl, Ranald Clouston, Rasmus E. Møgelberg, and
 Lars Birkedal, "Guarded Dependent Type Theory with Coinductive Types", 2016.
   https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.01586

 Containers motivate separating structural positions from their inhabitants.
 Guarded-recursion work explains why potentially infinite recursive behavior
 needs a productivity discipline stronger than ordinary operational laziness.

;;; Code:

import Control.Evaluation
import Control.Strict
import Control.Lazy
import Data.Functor
import Sequence

module Data.Sequence.Spine
  [Spine SNil SCons
   empty-spine singleton-spine cons-spine map-spine
   spine-from-list repeat-spine iterate-spine]


;;; Structure


data Spine e a
  = SNil
  | SCons a (e (Spine e a))


define empty-spine :: Spine e a
  SNil


define singleton-spine :: Evaluation e => a -> Spine e a
  value -> SCons value (defer (lambda (_) SNil))


define cons-spine :: a -> e (Spine e a) -> Spine e a
  value rest -> SCons value rest


define map-spine :: Functor e => (a -> b) -> Spine e a -> Spine e b
  _ SNil -> SNil
  f [SCons value rest] ->
    SCons (f value)
      (map (lambda (next) (map-spine f next)) rest)


instance Functor e => Functor (Spine e)
  map f values -> map-spine f values


instance Evaluation e => Sequence (Spine e a)
  type Element (Spine e a) = a
  type Strategy (Spine e a) = e

  uncons SNil -> nil
  uncons [SCons value rest] -> (value, rest)


instance ProductiveSequence (Spine Strict a)


instance FiniteSequence (Spine Strict a)


;;; Constructors


define spine-from-list :: Evaluation e => [a] -> Spine e a
  [] -> SNil
  [value|rest] ->
    SCons value (defer (lambda (_) (spine-from-list rest)))


define repeat-spine :: a -> Spine Lazy a
  value ->
    SCons value (defer (lambda (_) (repeat-spine value)))


define iterate-spine :: (a -> a) -> a -> Spine Lazy a
  next value ->
    SCons value
      (defer (lambda (_) (iterate-spine next (next value))))


;; The two constructors above are guarded values, but they do not justify a
;; blanket ProductiveSequence instance for every `Spine Lazy a`: callers can
;; still construct a Spine containing an arbitrary nonproductive Lazy tail.


define spine-lazy-head? :: Spine Lazy Int -> Int?
  SNil -> nil
  [SCons value _] -> value


define spine-lazy-second? :: Spine Lazy Int -> Int?
  SNil -> nil
  [SCons _ rest] -> spine-lazy-head? (force rest)


tests
  :infinite-construction:
  assert-eq (show (spine-lazy-second? (repeat-spine 7))) "7" "repeat-spine guards its recursive tail with Lazy"
  assert-eq (show (spine-lazy-second? (iterate-spine (lambda (x) (x + 1)) 4))) "5" "iterate-spine exposes successive lazy values"
  :infinite-construction:
